1201	Multiple $b$-tagging plays a central role since we require two $b$ jets from the boosted top decays and one or two additional $b$ jets from the spectator tops. Due to its large impact on the resulting sensitivity, a careful assessment on the $b$-tagging efficiency and associated jet-faking rate is required.
1202	
1203	In our semi-realistic $b$-tagging procedure, we assign a $b$-tag to each $r=0.4$ jet if there is a parton level $b$ or $c$ quark within $\Delta r=0.4$ from the jet axis, and we assume a $b$-tagging efficiency of
1204	\begin{equation}
1205		\epsilon_b = 0.70, \,\,\,\, \epsilon_c = 0.20, \,\,\,\,\, \epsilon_j = 0.01\,, 
1206	\end{equation}
1207	where $\epsilon_{b, c, j}$ are the efficiencies that a $b$, $c$ or a light jet will be tagged as a $b$-jet. 
1208	We note that in recent ATLAS analysis (Ref. \cite{ATLAS13TeV}), the following b-tagging efficiencies are used
1209	$\epsilon_b = 0.77$, $\epsilon_c = 0.22$, and $\epsilon_j = 0.0079$, which are slightly better than what we have used in our analysis.
1210	
1211	
1212	For a fat jet to be $b$-tagged, we require that a $b$-tagged $r=0.4$ jet lands within $\Delta R = R$ from the fat jet axis, where $R$ is the size of a fat jet. We take into account that more than one $b$-jet might land inside the fat jet, whereby we reweigh at least $1b$-tagging efficiencies of a fat jet depending on the $b$-tagging scheme described in Table \ref{tab:btag}.
1213	
1214	\begin{table}[htb]
1215	\begin{center}
1216	\scalebox{0.85}{
1217	\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
1218	\hline
1219		$b$-tagged score	                 &  Efficiency  (at least 1 $b$-tag)                                                                                                                               & value         \\ \hline
1220	 	 0 (jet: u,d,s,g)				& 	 	$\epsilon_j$				                                                                                                                &  0.01           \\  \hline
1221		1 (1c)					& 	 	$\epsilon_c$					                                                                                                        &  0.20	       \\  \hline
1222		2 (2c)					& 	 	$2\, \epsilon_c (1-\epsilon_c)+ {\epsilon_c}^2$		                                                                               &  0.36      \\  \hline
1223		3 (1b)					& 	 	$\epsilon_b$					                                                                                                         &  0.70	     \\  \hline
1224		4 (1b+1c)					& 	 	$\epsilon_b (1-\epsilon_c) + \epsilon_c (1-\epsilon_b)+\epsilon_b \epsilon_c$	                                     & 	0.76	     \\  \hline
1225		\multirow{2}{*}{ 5 (1b+2c)}		& 	 	$\epsilon_b (1-\epsilon_c)^2 + 2 (1-\epsilon_b) (1-\epsilon_c) \epsilon_c$ 		                                      & \multirow{2}{*}{0.81}   \\  
1226								&		$ +  2 \epsilon_b \epsilon_c (1- \epsilon_c) + \epsilon_c^2 (1-\epsilon_b) +\epsilon_b {\epsilon_c}^2$	    &	                                \\ \hline
1227		6 (2b)					& 	 	$2 \epsilon_b (1-\epsilon_b)+ {\epsilon_b}^2$			                                                                       &   0.91		   \\  \hline
1228		7 (2b+1c)					& 	 	$1 - (1-\epsilon_c) (1-\epsilon_b)^2$				                                                                                &   0.93		   \\  \hline
1229		8  (2b+2c)                     		& 	 	$1 - (1-\epsilon_c)^2 (1-\epsilon_b)^2$                                   			                                              &  0.94	   \\  \hline
1230		9  (3b)					& 	 	$1 - (1-\epsilon_b)^3$						                                                                                 &  0.97                         \\  \hline					 
1231	\end{tabular}\par}
1232	\caption{Efficiency that a top-tagged fat jet will be $b$-tagged assuming that it contains a specific number of light, $c$ or $b$ jets within $\Delta R = R$ from the jet axis, where $R$ is a size of a fat jet. $\epsilon_j$, $\epsilon_c$ and $\epsilon_b$ are $b$-tagging efficiencies for light, $c$ and $b$ jets respectively. We neglect the possibilities beyond three proper $b$-tagged jets within a fat jet.} \label{tab:btag} 
1233	\end{center}
1234	\end{table}
