1455	In the previous section, we managed to remove a substantial amount of the QCD background, except for the resilient $t\bar{t}$ (\text{hadronic}) + jets. This leads us to the semi-leptonic channel (cf. Figure \ref{fig:Diagram}) since $t\bar{t}$ (semi-leptonic) + jets now contains a single hadronic top (hardly expected to pass the ditop selection). On top of that, requiring an isolated lepton with mini-ISO $> 0.7$~\cite{Kaplan:2008ie} and $p_T^{\ell} > 25 \GeV$ safely removes any room for the gigantic QCD background, rendering relatively clean final states compared to the fully-hadronic channel (throughout the paper, we refer to ``leptons'' as muons and electrons only).
